1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Welcome to Module 02. Here we move from "what is deep learning" to "what is a neural network, mathematically, one neuron at a time."

2
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Three things run this module: the perceptron, the activation function, and the loss function. Those three ingredients — plus backprop — are the entire machinery.

3
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:32.000
A perceptron is a weighted sum plus a bias, passed through a non-linearity. Written out: y equals sigma of w dot x plus b. That's it. Everything else is stacking and training.

4
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:45.500
Why the non-linearity matters: without it, stacking layers collapses to a single linear map. No matter how deep you go, you'd still only fit a hyperplane. Non-linearities buy you expressivity.

5
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:58.000
The universal approximation theorem says a network with one hidden layer and enough neurons can approximate any continuous function on a compact set. But "enough" may be astronomical — depth is what makes this tractable.

6
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Activation choices: sigmoid and tanh saturate and kill gradients in deep nets. ReLU is the default — cheap, non-saturating on the positive side, but it can "die" when a neuron outputs zero forever. Leaky ReLU and GELU are the common fixes.

7
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Softmax sits at the output of multi-class classifiers. It turns arbitrary logits into a probability simplex. Pair it with categorical cross-entropy, not MSE — the gradients are far better behaved.

8
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Loss functions encode what "good" means. MSE for regression. Binary cross-entropy for two-class. Categorical cross-entropy for multi-class. Huber for outlier-robust regression. Your loss is your contract with the optimizer.

9
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Backpropagation is not magic — it's the chain rule applied to a computation graph, cached so you don't redo shared work. Forward pass stores activations. Backward pass multiplies local Jacobians from the loss back to every parameter.

10
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:08.000
A practical mental model: every trainable tensor has a gradient. SGD updates it by minus learning rate times that gradient. Everything else — Adam, RMSProp, momentum — is a clever way to denoise or rescale that step.

11
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:22.000
In Keras the Sequential API is a straight stack — input, a few Dense layers, an output. It's the right tool for 80% of classification and regression problems.

12
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:36.000
The Functional API exposes the computation graph. You build layers as callable objects on tensors, and you can fork, merge, share weights. Use it for residual connections, multi-input or multi-output models, and anything non-linear in topology.

13
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Subclassing Model gives you a custom call method and full imperative control. Reach for it only when Functional cannot express your topology — custom dynamic behavior, autoregressive decoders, meta-learning.

14
00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Normalization layers — BatchNorm, LayerNorm — stabilize training by reparameterizing activations. BatchNorm across the batch dimension, LayerNorm across the feature dimension. LayerNorm is the sequence-model default because it doesn't depend on batch size.

15
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Weight initialization matters more than beginners expect. Glorot for tanh-family activations, He for ReLU-family. Bad init can make deep networks refuse to train at all.

16
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:35.000
The custom training loop. Keras gives you model.fit for the common case, but GradientTape lets you write the forward-loss-backward-update dance yourself. That's the door to GANs, reinforcement learning, and research work.

17
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:50.000
The three checks before you trust a trained network: training loss decreased, validation loss didn't explode, the predictions pass a sanity baseline. Skip these and you will ship bugs that look like models.

18
00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:05.000
Common first-network bugs: forgetting to normalize inputs, using the wrong loss for the task, mismatched output layer shape, and confusing logits with probabilities when computing cross-entropy.

19
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:20.000
The labs for this module walk you through each piece. You'll hand-code backprop on a two-layer net before touching Keras. That one exercise pays for the rest of the course.

20
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:35.000
Next module we move from fully-connected networks to convolutional networks — the architecture that first let deep learning dominate computer vision. See you there.
